hi there steve kaufman here back in  vancouver the weather's nice it's a  little cooler than palm springs  uh today i want to talk about the  importance of face-to-face  contact in language learning and the  reason for that is that i had an  exchange on twitter i made some comment  about language learning and a teacher  came on and said  there's too much emphasis on technology  the  most important thing is face-to-face  contact  in the classroom  so i disagree  i think that face-to-face contact  as a part of language learning is  relatively unimportant  i think language learning is a much more  individual  activity  obviously when you are reading you are  not making face-to-face contact with  anyone when i'm listening to my mp3  player i'm not making face-to-face  contact  uh if i'm studying at link i'm not  making face-to-face contact if i'm  watching movies i'm not making  face-to-face content  there's so many things  that you can do as an individual you  don't forget lincoln you can go down to  the library  and borrow free of charge  you know a wide range of learning  materials for whatever language you're  learning  so much of  what is needed in order to acquire a  language  relies on you the learner relies on you  doing things on your own things that you  find interesting and the face-to-face  contact becomes  a reward in a way it's kind of like the  goal obviously at some point in my  arabic learning i want to be in  situations where i am in face-to-face  contact with arabic speaking people and  i can converse and  and enjoy that whole experience but  there is so much work that i have to put  in  on my own  before that in order that this  face-to-face contact is going to be  meaningful enjoyable and so forth and  you can have face-to-face contact in the  classroom and in fact the  sort of language  content the  target language the language you're  learning you know the intensity of that  language environment could be quite low  it could be that the teacher is  explaining something it could be that  you're listening to other students who  speak the language very poorly  and i much prefer the you know  hearing the pure language hearing  stories hearing things of interest in  the language or reading  and getting  used to the language because  the language is not something that's  going to be taught or explained and then  you've got it aha now i understand how i  can speak the language to learn a  language requires so much exposure it's  a slow gradual process of allowing your  brain to get used to a different way  of expressing things different  structures different words and this  requires a lot of time  with you the learner  and the language you're learning  not in some face-to-face even these you  know conversation clubs and so forth  i've always felt you know in japan for  example that ego kaiwa have a little  group that speaks english  that's not going to do it because again  you're in these ego kaiwa  where we have five or six japanese  people and and a foreign native speaker  speaking they're using a very limited  range of vocabulary and and essentially  the job is and of course the intensity  of that language exposure is not as high  as  listening and reading and acquiring  words now as i've always said at some  point you have to start speaking and you  have to start start speaking a lot and  you have to start using the words that  you have acquired at the very least to  hear them in some meaningful context in  an exchange with someone but that comes  later on  it can start early but it becomes a  bigger and bigger part of your language  learning later on initially there's this  major investment to be made by the  individual  and it's and i think someone once said  well you know reading is very  you know as social and i like  interacting with people i like face to  face and this teacher was saying the  same thing in a bit of little twitter  exchange that we have  um  actually it's not anti-social to read  you are interacting with thoughts you  are interacting with words you are  interacting with the language and all of  this is helping you to get used to the  language so uh the idea that somehow you  have to have the space to face contact  face face-to-face contact with the  teacher  at some point it can be useful it's not  inexpensive to organize  uh it's far better to put a lot of  effort into things that are less  expensive  build up your capability so that when  you have the face-to-face exchange you  can really take advantage of it  i should add that face-to-face  uh needn't actually be face-to-face i  just did a  lesson for skype with one of through  skype with one of our link members an  english lesson and we didn't have our  video on we just spoke and it was like a  telephone conversation through skype  and i think even there i think it  sometimes can be  you know maybe you focus better on the  words sometimes when you actually don't  see the other person's face not that i'm  against seeing people's faces and at  some point yeah  we definitely want to have face-to-face  encounters with people but it's not a  key to learning the language and so  that's really what i wanted to say about  the importance of face-to-face contact  as  you know a part of your language  learning strategy it's a part of it  it needn't be a major part of it it's  certainly not a condition of learning  the language  so thank you for listening bye for now  [Music]  you
 

